police are growing increasingly  concerned about a situation that's going  on in our schools 8 news now is learned  wednesday someone called 9-1-1 sent a  text about a gun at southeast career  technical academy police of course  responded so it turned out the  information someone had that a gun was  on campus was fake so was the call for  help it's called swatting when someone  makes a false call to 9-1-1 or another  person about a threat big enough to get  a large presence response to come to a  location kate houston join us now after  talking to police about the impact and  kate the consequences of swatting yes  well this is a problem for law  enforcement really across the nation now  you can be charged with seeking a false  9-1-1 call you can also be charged with  making a terroristic threat and bottom  line law enforcement say it's not a joke  swatting has gained national attention  for leading to innocent deaths and long  jail sentences there have been some very  results of this lieutenant brian zink  with ccsd pd says they've already dealt  with swatting at a few schools across  the valley depending on the type of call  and how fast we can authenticate it it  may take minutes or may take hours to  make sure that it is a fake call and not  true at all in addition to taking police  officers away from legitimate  emergencies it can also cause extreme  emotional distress for families involved  because of cell phones and social media  misinformation like this spreads very  quickly because you know we know that  there are kids inside the school that as  soon as the lockdown is initiated they  start texting their parents nine control  un channel articles zinc says they don't  stop investigating either using assets  from the federal government and other  agencies we look for certain keywords  that are mentioned that have been  mentioned in past swatting  investigations that we've done or  certain phone numbers or area codes or  location where the phone call does come  from now police are encouraging parents  to sit down with their kids and talk  about the impact this could have on  their lives it seems like it's funny  it's just a joke it's not serious but it  is very serious and it can have dire  consequences on your future  at this time school district police and  metro are investigating who made the  call and sent that text message  currently there are no federal or state  laws against swatting in 2019 the  anti-swatting act was introduced to  congress to increase those penalties but  it has remained in review back to you  brian
 

